[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"@Injectable Decorator and its Metadata in Angular"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Injectable decorator in Angular marks a class as available to be provided and injected as a dependency. It's crucial for enabling Angular's dependency injection (DI) system. This tutorial will cover the purpose, usage, and metadata associated with @Injectable."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Purpose of @Injectable"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The primary purpose of @Injectable is to signal to Angular's DI system that a class can be injected into other components, directives, services, or even other injectable services. Without @Injectable, Angular may not be able to properly resolve the dependencies of a class, especially when dealing with constructor parameters that themselves have dependencies."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Usage"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class MyService {
  constructor() { }

  getData(): string {
    return 'Data from MyService';
  }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, @Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' }) marks MyService as injectable. The providedIn: 'root' configuration registers the service as a singleton at the application level, meaning Angular creates only one instance of MyService and shares it throughout the application."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Metadata: providedIn"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Injectable decorator accepts a metadata object, which currently primarily consists of the 'providedIn' property. This property specifies the scope in which the service should be provided."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'root': Provides a single, shared instance of the service throughout the entire application. This is the most common and recommended approach for most services."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'any': Creates a unique instance in every module that imports the service. This is generally discouraged as it can lead to unexpected behavior."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"NgModule: You can specify a particular NgModule to provide the service. This makes the service available only within that module."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"@Injectable({
  providedIn: MyModule
})
export class MyService { ... }"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Injecting the Service"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { MyService } from './my.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-my-component',
  template: `<p>{{ data }}</p>`,
})
export class MyComponent {
  data: string;

  constructor(private myService: MyService) {
    this.data = this.myService.getData();
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this component, MyService is injected into the constructor. Angular's DI system resolves the dependency and provides an instance of MyService to the component. The component then uses the service to retrieve and display data."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"When is @Injectable Required?"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"@Injectable is technically only required when a service has its own dependencies. However, it's considered best practice to always use @Injectable for services, even if they don't currently have dependencies. This ensures that the service is ready for dependency injection in the future without requiring code changes."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using @Injectable"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enables Dependency Injection: Allows Angular to manage the creation and injection of service instances."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Testability: Makes it easier to mock and test services in isolation."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Promotes the creation of reusable services that can be injected into multiple components."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Loose Coupling: Reduces dependencies between components and services, making the application more maintainable."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Injectable decorator is a fundamental part of Angular's dependency injection system. By understanding its purpose and usage, you can create more modular, testable, and maintainable Angular applications. Remember to always use @Injectable for services and configure the 'providedIn' property appropriately to manage the service's scope."}]
